  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1132 AM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nThe majority of the week is expected to feature broad midlevel \nridging over the eastern CONUS, which will keep temperatures 5-10 \ndegrees above normal for Middle TN. However, \"normal\" \ntemperatures for this time of year are around 70 degrees in the \nafternoon, so temperatures in the mid to upper 70s is anomalous \nbut not uncomfortably warm. Humidity remains low through the week \nas well. With no rain in sight through the end of the week, \nareawide drought conditions are expected to worsen.\n\nThings are finally starting to come into better agreement on a\npattern shift next weekend. Long range ensembles finally hint at\nsome long-awaited rain as early as Saturday night as a broad \nlongwave trough approaches from the Plains. LREF probabilities of \nmeasurable rainfall (0.01\") are still at around 30-40% Saturday \nnight via the grand ensemble, increasing significantly on Sunday.\nIts individual members have starkly different solutions with the \nENS showing a much faster onset and the GEFS pushing precip onset \nseveral hours back into Sunday. Either way, this is not a strong \ncold front next weekend by any means, just some light rain \nassociated with a broad upper level trough.",
